<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gobir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gobir was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdom in present-day north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>. Its sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>'s hostility to dan Fodio triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of 1804, and the storming of the Gobir capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a> in 1808 consolidated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gobir was one of the seven original kingdoms of Hausaland and the northernmost of them, relied on to shield its sister states from northern enemies, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>. Broader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> tradition names its founder as Duma, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a>, but the records of the ruling house trace its descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> led by a first king, Bana Turmi. The historians S. J. Hogben and A. H. M. Kirk-Greene noted that some think this claim was invented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawa-jan-gwarzo">Bawa Jan Gwarzo</a> (r. 1777–1795) to avoid paying tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> described Gobir's many herding villages and its weavers and artisans, whose textiles and shoes reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and Gao. Constant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> pressure pushed the Gobirawa south from Asben to Maigali, then Goran Rami and, in the early 18th century, Birnin Lalle. Sarkin Gobir Soba besieged Zabarma for three years, raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a> and was finally killed fighting Agadez. His successor Uban Iche was murdered by his son Babari (1742–1770), who sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birnin-zamfara">Birnin Zamfara</a> and built a new walled capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>, on the Gulbin Rima River.</p><p>Sarki Bawa appears to have invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> to Gobir in 1774, and dan Fodio settled at Degel and preached against the abuses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> elite. Later rulers turned against him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, after failing to have him killed, exiled him and his followers. Resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> went on in the north-east, and in 1836 Sarki Mayaki built a new capital at Tibiri, 10 km north of Maradi, but Sultan Muhammed Bello crushed a Gobir revolt that year at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gawakuke">Battle of Gawakuke</a>. In 1975 a reunited traditional sultanate took up residence in Sabon Birni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Gobir followed the sarauta system of kingship. The sarki was chosen by an electoral college called the Taran Gobir, the Gobir nine, whose members had to agree unanimously and also served as his council. After the sarki the highest titleholder was the ubandawaki, commander of the cavalry. Officials at court represented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of blacksmiths, weavers, dyers and other crafts, the indigenous groups and the immigrant communities, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> and the Fulbe, together with the mallamai, the Islamic scholars. The Inna, usually the sarki's sister, was high priestess of the Bori cult and served the dynastic goddess Inna Baka.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gobir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Daura and married its queen; their descendants were said to have founded the seven ‘legitimate’ states, the Hausa Bakwai of Daura, Kano, Katsina, Zazzau (Zaria), <b>Gobir</b>, Rano and Biram, while seven other states, the Banza Bakwai, including Zamfara, Kebbi, Yauri and Nupe, were considered less legitimate offshoots.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars with Katsina, Zamfara and <b>Gobir</b> filled much of the following century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founder, Usman dan Fodio, born in 1754 in <b>Gobir</b>, in what is now north-western Nigeria, was a Fulani scholar of the Qadiriyya Sufi order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gobir</b>, one of the Hausa Bakwai, is believed to have formed further north, near the Air massif, and moved south under Tuareg pressure.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gobir">Kingdom of Gobir</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1836 <b>Gobir</b> and Maradi forces were defeated by Muhammad Bello at the battle of Gawakuke, and the Gobir ruler was killed, but the war continued for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-tsibiri">Sultanate of Tsibiri</a></li></ul></section>
